# LUCI image-prompt rules — custom photography, not AI stock

**When to use:** whenever writing or revising an image-generation prompt (ChatGPT, Midjourney, or any generator) for a LUCI marketing asset — website industry/persona pages, sales decks, social, proposals, portal, case studies, anywhere a photo or illustration is needed. The goal: every generated image must look like LUCI commissioned and shot the photo ourselves.

**Reference library.** Real client photography lives at `/Users/janehaynie/Library/CloudStorage/OneDrive-LUCISystems/Marketing - Documents/Content/Project Media` (Rio Rack Room, Sams Town LED, Tachi LEDs, Osage LED Wall, Aliante LED, plus `OLD/` → Ameristar, San Manuel, Stanly Ranch, UTE, Northern Quest). Consult it before writing any casino prompt; replicate the *environments*, never use the files themselves. The website images under `luci-website/public/images/industries/...` are AI-generated, NOT reference — do not treat them as ground truth.

## 0. Prime directive

Every image must look like LUCI commissioned and shot the photo ourselves — custom project photography, not AI-generated stock. If a viewer can tell it's AI, the prompt failed. Bias every choice toward "a real photographer stood here with a real camera," not "an AI rendered this."

## 1. Kill the AI tells

No over-polished perfection, no impossible even lighting, no "epic cinematic" glow, no oversaturated HDR, no plastic skin, no generic futuristic blue/purple haze, no centered hero object on an empty background, no perfect symmetry, no random extra fingers. Avoid the "Midjourney look" entirely. When in doubt, add imperfection.

## 2. Authenticity markers (from the real LUCI photos)

Include real-world imperfection: dust, cable tangles, in-progress elements (plastic sheeting, scissor lifts, stacked chairs, monitors showing a real OS desktop), motion-blurred screens, ceiling vents/HVAC, scuffed floors. Real materials and textures — dark wood paneling, swirling casino carpet, polished wood counters, leather upholstery, metal racks, conduit, acoustic felt, subway tile. Practical, motivated, in-situ lighting — recessed spotlights, warm incandescent pools, screen/LED glow as a light source — never studio or "rim-lit hero" lighting. Mix warm and cool.

## 3. People — at work and in the space

Two kinds of people appear in LUCI photography, both candid and never posed:

- **LUCI techs doing real work** (the bulk of the work): a technician in a LUCI-logo t-shirt deep in a rack, a worker on a scissor lift, someone focused on a tablet. Focused/strained expressions, not smiling-at-camera. Branding stays subtle — a small logo on a shirt or a rack label, never the subject of the photo.
- **Real customers/guests inside a space** (also needed, do not rule these out): patrons at a sportsbook, guests in a hotel lobby, fans in an arena concourse, travelers in a terminal, attendees in a convention hall. Mid-activity — watching a screen, walking, seated, in conversation — never smiling-at-camera or posing.

For both: prefer over-the-shoulder, hands, back-of-head, or candid-at-a-distance to dodge face-generation issues. When faces show, specify natural, specific, non-model people with real expressions. No fashion models, no "diverse group smiling at the lens" stock cliché.

## 4. Camera & lens discipline

Wide-angle 14–24mm for spaces (scale + leading lines), deep depth of field, architectural/site-documentation perspective, eye-level or low. Tight high-angle for rack work. Always name a focal length, a DoF, and "shot on a real body/lens" (e.g., Sony A7III, 16-35mm f/4) to anchor realism and natural wide-angle distortion + mild vignetting.

## 5. Lighting discipline

Practical and motivated, never theatrical. Warm pools against deep shadow (high contrast), screen/LED glow as the key light source in AV spaces, mixed warm/cool. No flat even studio light, no purple-orange gradient, no rim-lit subject glow. Let the room's real fixtures and screens do the lighting.

## 6. Composition

Leading lines, real depth (foreground / midground / background), real architectural detail. No centered-subject-on-empty-bg. Use negative space the way a real photographer does — to show scale, not to "feature" a product.

## 7. Palette & materials

Grounded in real materials (dark woods, carpet, metal, tile, leather) with saturated pops **only** from screens/LEDs (greens, blues, oranges) — never from ambient color grading. LUCI mint/gold may appear as screen content or subtle branding, never as ambient room color.

## 8. Branding & discretion

LUCI logo subtle (shirt, rack label, screen), never a giant centered logo. No fake client names, no percentage claims. Per the voice rule — institutions, not adjectives; show what the platform does, never how the software "feels."

## 9. Specificity beats generality

Name real, specific details — the football-field placemat and floral centerpiece, the swirling red/black carpet pattern, the chevron wood floor, the forest-green subway tile, the scissor lift, the Windows desktop on the counter. Specificity is what kills the "generic AI" read. Generic = failed. If a detail could belong to any property, it's wrong; make it belong to *this* property.

## 10. Industry grounding

- **Casino** = replicate real LUCI casino environments (rack rooms, sportsbooks, LED video walls, odds boards, betting counters, signage tickers, floor infrastructure) from the reference library.
- **Hotels / sports / airports / conference** = no real client photos exist, so imagine equivalent real spaces in the **same** authentic style — a real hotel ballroom mid-turnover, a real arena control room, a real terminal concourse, a real convention hall divisible wall — with real materials, signage, lighting, and in-progress detail. Do not invent a generic "futuristic hotel"; invent a *specific* real one.

## 11. Casino anti-clichés

LUCI's casino is the **operations** side, not the gambling glamour. No roulette close-ups, no fanned chips, no Vegas-skyline bokeh, no card-dealer hands. Shoot the infrastructure: rack rooms, sportsbook video walls, odds boards, betting counters, signage tickers, floor management, before/after transformations. That's the LUCI casino.

## 12. Output format & genre

Photographic, photojournalistic / architectural-documentation / site-photography genre. 35mm or medium-format, real lens characteristics. No illustration, no 3D render, no "digital art," no painting. State an aspect ratio per use (hero poster vs. card thumbnail).

## 13. Negative-prompt discipline

Every prompt ships with a negative prompt: `generic stock photo, polished, sterile, perfect symmetry, cinematic, epic, futuristic, blue haze, purple haze, HDR, oversaturated, illustration, 3D render, posed model, stock-photo cliché, smiling at camera, watermark, text overlay` (unless branding is intentional).

## 14. Workflow

1. Check the reference library for the relevant industry (casino has real photos; others don't).
2. Decide the shot: which environment, which moment of work or use, which lens and light.
3. Write the prompt with §1–§13 applied — specific details, real lens, practical light, authenticity markers.
4. Append the §13 negative prompt.
5. Note the aspect ratio for the use.
6. If the result still reads AI, diagnose which tell survived and re-prompt against that specific tell.
